Each poll returned at most 10 records, and the positions moved the moment it returned. The keys sent 12, 9 and 9 payments to the three partitions (Producers I showed how a key picks a partition). Each empty poll waited the full 500 ms, so seven of them fill the rest of the 4 seconds. Your first line may say `poll 1:  0 records   positions {}` instead: the consumer was still joining its group and had no partitions yet. The second run writes 30 more payments and reads only those. It starts at the committed offsets, 12, 9 and 9 (`poll 1: 10 records   positions {0=22, 1=9, 2=9}`), and ends at 24, 18 and 18.

